    "updated_at": "2025-11-18T15:58:36Z",
    "body": "> #5440 for instance.\n\nThanks.\n\nAs far as I can tell, this didn't change \"scripting is enabled\" for sandboxed documents.\n\nhttps://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/5440/files#diff-41cf6794ba4200b839c53531555f0f3998df4cbb01a4d5cb0b94e3ca5e23947dR85856-R85859 changed the suggested granularity for which a user setting could be applied. Maybe this part should be reverted if the goal is more consistent parsing as seen by scripts.\n\n> I don't like the idea of only following a completely different code path if the end user has enabled some setting. That doesn't seem like it will lead to a more robust system.\n\nI think it would be more robust because you're either in a world where scripting is enabled for the purpose of `noscript`, or scripting is disabled for the purpose of `noscript`. Today it can be either for different documents, which is unexpected and is what enables the XSS attacks.",
